\h Haggai
\toc1 Haggai
\toc2 Haggai
\mt1 Haggai
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 God’s command to rebuild the temple
\p
\v 1 In Darius’ second year as king \add of Persia\add*, on the 1st of the sixth month, the prophet Haggai brought Yahweh’s message to the governor of Judah, Zerubbabel (Shealtiel’s son), and to the high priest, Joshua Yehotsadak’s son), telling \add them that\add*
\v 2 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “These people say that it’s not the right time to rebuild Yahweh’s residence.”
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh gave this message to the prophet Haggai \add to tell the people\add*:
\m
\v 4 Is it a time for all of you to live in your panelled houses, while Yahweh’s temple lies in ruins?
\v 5 So now Commander-in-chief Yahweh says: “Decide what you’re all going to do.
\v 6 You’ve all planted a lot, but only harvested a little. You’ve eaten, but it never fills you. You all drink, but never enough to satisfy you. You put on clothes, but never feel warm enough. You earn wages, but your pockets seem to be full of holes.”
\p
\v 7 So Commander-in-chief Yahweh says \add again\add*: “Decide what you’re all going to do.
\v 8 Go up into the hills and bring back timber to build the temple. This will please and honour me,” says Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 “You expected much, but \add gained\add* little. Anything you brought home, I blew away \add again\add*. Why? Commander-in-chief Yahweh says it’s because my residence is still in ruins, while you’re all \add busy\add* working on your own houses.
\v 10 That’s why the sky withholds the dew and the soil withholds its crops.
\v 11 I’ve summoned a drought onto the land and into the hills, onto the grain and the new wine, onto the oil and crops from the ground, onto \add both\add* people and livestock, and onto everything you all do.”
\s1 The people start rebuilding
\p
\v 12 Then Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel and Yehotsadak’s son Joshua, the high priest, and all the rest of the people listened to the voice of their god Yahweh via the words of the prophet Haggai, because Yahweh their god had sent him, and the people respected Yahweh.
\v 13 Then Yahweh’s messenger Haggai passed this message from Yahweh onto the people, “Yahweh declares that I’m with you all.”
\v 14 Then Yahweh inspired Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel, the governor of Judah, inspired Yehotsadak’s son Joshua, the high priest, and inspired all the rest of the people, and they came and started work on the temple for their god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\v 15 on the twenty-fourth day of the sixth month of the second year of Darius the king \add of Persia\add*.
\c 2
\s1 The splendour of the new temple
\p
\v 1 On the 21st of the seventh month \add (about a month later)\add*, Yahweh spoke \add again\add* through the prophet Haggai:
\v 2 Please ask Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel, the governor of Judah, and ask Yehotsadak’s son Joshua, the high priest, and ask the rest of the people,
\v 3 “Are there any of you still alive who saw the splendour of the former temple? How does it look to you now? It must now seem pretty much like \+em nothing\+em* in comparison.
\v 4 Yahweh is telling you now, Zerubbabel, to be strong. And be strong, high priest Joshua, and be strong all you people of the land. Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares that I’m with you as you work.
\v 5 That’s what I promised your ancestors when they came out of Egypt, and my spirit remains among you. Don’t be afraid,
\v 6 because Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that in a little while, I’ll shake the heavens and the earth, the sea and the dry land, once more.
\v 7 I’ll shake all the nations, and they’ll come \add here bringing\add* their treasure. Then I’ll fill this temple with \add my\add* splendour, says Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\v 8 Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares that the gold and silver \add belong\add* to me.
\v 9 I declare that this temple will be greater in the future than it was in the past, and \add also\add* that I will give peace and prosperity to this place.”
\s1 Haggai consults the priests
\p
\v 10 On the 24th of the ninth month in \add King\add* Darius’ second year \add (about two months later)\add*, Yahweh gave a message to the prophet Haggai:
\v 11 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “Ask the priests about Moses’ instructions.
\v 12 ‘If a priest took some meat that had been offered to God and carried it wrapped in a piece of clothing, then if the clothing touched some other food, would that other food become holy?’ ”
\p “No, it wouldn’t,” the priests replied.
\p
\v 13 Then Haggai asked, “\add But\add* if a person became unclean by touching a corpse and then touched any of that \add food\add*, would it become unclean?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* it would become unclean,” the priests answered.
\p
\v 14 “That’s what Yahweh declares about you people,” Haggai continued. “\add He says that\add* that’s how this country acts towards him. Your actions are dishonourable, and then that same disrespect transfers to your offerings.
\v 15 So now think back to before stones were being laid for Yahweh’s temple.
\v 16 During that time, when someone went to get twenty \add containers of grain\add*, there were \add only\add* ten there, and when someone went to fill fifty \add jars of wine\add* from the vat, there was \add only enough for\add* twenty.
\v 17 Yahweh declares that he caused blight and mildew and hail to affect your work, but you \add still\add* didn’t turn to him.
\v 18 Think back to the time from when the foundation of Yahweh’s temple was laid, until today (this 24th of the ninth month). Consider that.
\v 19 Is \add any grain\add* left in the storehouse for seed? \add What’s more,\add* the vines, and the fig trees and pomegranate trees and olive trees, haven’t produced fruit. \add However,\add* Yahweh will bless you from today \add onwards\add*.”
\s1 God’s promise to Zerubbabel
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh gave a second message to Haggai on the 24th:
\v 21 Tell Zerubbabel, the governor of Judah, “I’m \add about to\add* shake the heavens and the earth.
\v 22 I’ll overthrow the thrones of kingdoms, and destroy the strength of the nations. I’ll overthrow chariots and riders—horses and their riders will fall and \add related tribes\add* will kill each other.
\v 23 Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares that on that day he will take Shealtiel’s son Zerubbabel and cause him to place Yahweh’s mark on the nation like a signet ring, because he’s been chosen.”
